1
00:00:50,134 --> 00:00:55,389
(BABY CRYING)

2
00:01:06,817 --> 00:01:09,737
This is Mr. Mathison. He's a doctor.

3
00:01:11,072 --> 00:01:12,782
Are you OK?

4
00:01:12,865 --> 00:01:15,201
An ambulance is on the way.

5
00:01:15,284 --> 00:01:16,827
Thank you.

6
00:01:23,584 --> 00:01:25,294
Is there a name yet?

7
00:01:26,879 --> 00:01:28,297
Elijah.

8
00:01:28,381 --> 00:01:30,758
(BABY CRYING LOUDLY)

9
00:01:33,344 --> 00:01:36,430
Is he supposed to be crying like this?

10
00:01:36,514 --> 00:01:37,848
May I?

11
00:01:43,688 --> 00:01:45,690
Shh, shh, shh, shh...

12
00:01:45,773 --> 00:01:47,775
(BABY CONTINUES CRYING)

13
00:01:53,656 --> 00:01:55,283
Can I have him back?

14
00:02:00,371 --> 00:02:02,790
What happened during the delivery?

15
00:02:02,873 --> 00:02:05,668
Nothing. It was very quick.

16
00:02:05,751 --> 00:02:07,753
The baby just wanted
to come right out.

17
00:02:07,837 --> 00:02:09,630
There were no problems.

18
00:02:11,841 --> 00:02:14,176
Did you drop him?

19
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:15,469
What?

20
00:02:16,304 --> 00:02:18,597
Did you drop this baby?

21
00:02:18,681 --> 00:02:20,725
Jesus Christ, no.

22
00:02:25,896 --> 00:02:28,816
Inform the ambulance
that we have a situation.

23
00:02:28,899 --> 00:02:31,902
- All right.
- Ma'am.

24
00:02:31,986 --> 00:02:35,031
I've never seen... this.

25
00:02:37,742 --> 00:02:41,787
It appears that your baby
has sustained some fractures

26
00:02:41,871 --> 00:02:43,873
while inside your uterus.

27
00:02:47,043 --> 00:02:49,503
His arms and his legs are broken.

28
00:02:50,212 --> 00:02:52,965
(SOBBING)

29
00:02:56,844 --> 00:03:00,681
(MUSIC PLAYING)

30
00:03:39,512 --> 00:03:41,764
MAN OVER PA: <i>This is Eastrail 177.</i>

31
00:03:41,889 --> 00:03:43,891
<i>Next stop, Philadelphia.</i>

32
00:03:48,854 --> 00:03:51,899
(GIRL HUMMING)

33
00:04:16,590 --> 00:04:17,842
(DOOR CLOSES)

34
00:04:17,925 --> 00:04:19,135
WOMAN: Are you alone?

35
00:04:21,387 --> 00:04:24,056
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

36
00:04:55,504 --> 00:04:58,090
(TRAIN ENTERS TUNNEL)

37
00:05:09,477 --> 00:05:11,854
Someone left this magazine.

38
00:05:11,937 --> 00:05:13,814
Want to take a look at it?

39
00:05:13,898 --> 00:05:15,983
Can I take a look at that one?

40
00:05:22,114 --> 00:05:24,074
Thanks.

41
00:05:24,158 --> 00:05:26,035
You like sports?

42
00:05:26,118 --> 00:05:27,286
It's my field.

43
00:05:27,369 --> 00:05:29,622
I represent athletes. I'm an agent.

44
00:05:30,789 --> 00:05:34,001
Are you looking for any male
synchronized swimmers?

45
00:05:34,084 --> 00:05:36,420
I'm thinking about getting into that.

46
00:05:36,504 --> 00:05:38,005
Is that right?

47
00:05:40,883 --> 00:05:44,470
I'm afraid of water.
Think that's a problem?

48
00:05:52,394 --> 00:05:55,189
You represent someone
in Philadelphia?

49
00:05:55,272 --> 00:05:57,733
I'm meeting this player
from Temple University.

50
00:05:57,816 --> 00:05:59,860
He's a cornerback.

51
00:05:59,944 --> 00:06:03,697
This kid is 6'1" ,210 pounds.

52
00:06:03,822 --> 00:06:07,952
He runs a 40 in 4.3 seconds.
He's gonna be a god.

53
00:06:08,035 --> 00:06:09,745
Do you like football?

54
00:06:10,955 --> 00:06:12,540
Not really.

55
00:06:24,260 --> 00:06:26,053
I'm David Dunn.

56
00:06:26,971 --> 00:06:28,389
- Kelly.
- Hi.

57
00:06:28,472 --> 00:06:30,683
Hi, nice to meet you.

58
00:06:30,766 --> 00:06:32,977
Nice... Nice to meet you.

59
00:06:40,442 --> 00:06:43,404
How long are you gonna be
in Philadelphia?

60
00:06:50,869 --> 00:06:52,788
I'm married.

61
00:06:52,871 --> 00:06:54,081
Great.

62
00:06:54,164 --> 00:06:55,708
I'm sorry.

63
00:06:55,791 --> 00:06:57,418
What are you talking about?

64
00:07:02,381 --> 00:07:04,425
I think you misunderstood.

65
00:07:04,550 --> 00:07:06,093
What I was...

66
00:07:07,386 --> 00:07:09,096
- ...saying...
- I know.

67
00:07:11,181 --> 00:07:13,976
I think I'm going to find
another seat.

68
00:07:14,059 --> 00:07:16,729
I... I didn't mean...

69
00:08:06,945 --> 00:08:09,365
(TRAIN WHISTLE BLOWING)

70
00:08:20,918 --> 00:08:23,003
(TRAIN WHISTLE CONTINUES)

71
00:08:30,969 --> 00:08:34,473
(TRAIN RATTLING)

72
00:08:49,655 --> 00:08:52,157
(COMMERCIAL JINGLE PLAYING)

73
00:08:56,036 --> 00:08:59,748
<i>That's what he fail to realize.
I look classy, not trashy.</i>

74
00:08:59,832 --> 00:09:01,083
<i>I look classy too.</i>

75
00:09:01,166 --> 00:09:03,419
<i>What about me?
Don't I look classy?</i>

76
00:09:04,253 --> 00:09:06,797
<i>But I'm afraid you forgot... this!</i>

77
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:07,923
<i>And this!</i>

78
00:09:08,006 --> 00:09:11,135
MAN: <i>Where has you been, Banana?</i>

79
00:09:11,218 --> 00:09:13,303
MAN 2: <i>Now I've got you, Weasel.</i>

80
00:09:13,387 --> 00:09:15,389
MAN 3: <i>Some cars are still on fire,</i>

81
00:09:15,472 --> 00:09:18,142
<i>and the debris is everywhere.</i>

82
00:09:18,225 --> 00:09:19,518
<i>We are not sure at this time</i>

83
00:09:19,601 --> 00:09:22,479
<i>how rescue personnel will be
able to get on scene at all.</i>

84
00:09:22,563 --> 00:09:24,189
MAN 4: <i>If you're just tuning in,</i>

85
00:09:24,273 --> 00:09:27,985
<i>Eastrail train number 177
has derailed outside Philadelphia.</i>

86
00:09:28,068 --> 00:09:30,195
<i>We're bringing you
live coverage from Skycam 4.</i>

87
00:09:30,279 --> 00:09:34,032
MAN 3: <i>That appears to be a rescue
ambulance. There are more behind it.</i>

88
00:09:34,116 --> 00:09:35,534
<i>I can see on the access road.</i>

89
00:09:35,617 --> 00:09:38,495
<i>I don't know how close they're
gonna be able to get, David.</i>

90
00:09:38,579 --> 00:09:40,706
<i>These are woods and... trees.</i>

91
00:09:40,789 --> 00:09:43,792
<i>It's gonna be a very difficult
rescue effort.</i>

92
00:09:52,217 --> 00:09:55,137
- (MONITOR BEEPING)
- (LABORED BREATHING)

93
00:09:59,224 --> 00:10:01,143
MAN: Hack some of that off.

94
00:10:14,323 --> 00:10:15,866
Hi.

95
00:10:15,949 --> 00:10:18,076
I'm Dr. Dubin.

96
00:10:18,160 --> 00:10:21,997
You're in the emergency room
in the Philadelphia City Hospital.

97
00:10:22,080 --> 00:10:23,957
You were in a serious accident.

98
00:10:25,209 --> 00:10:27,169
Look at me.

99
00:10:27,669 --> 00:10:29,004
How are you feeling?

100
00:10:31,215 --> 00:10:33,467
- OK.
- Good.

101
00:10:33,550 --> 00:10:36,053
I'm gonna ask you some questions.

102
00:10:36,136 --> 00:10:39,223
Have you ever had any heart
or asthma problems in the past?

103
00:10:40,307 --> 00:10:41,308
No.

104
00:10:41,391 --> 00:10:43,268
Kidney or renal problems?

105
00:10:44,436 --> 00:10:45,687
No.

106
00:10:45,771 --> 00:10:47,189
Any allergies?

107
00:10:49,316 --> 00:10:50,943
No.

108
00:10:51,026 --> 00:10:54,196
(MONITOR BEEPS FASTER)

109
00:10:55,948 --> 00:10:58,200
Where were you sitting on the train?

110
00:11:02,162 --> 00:11:05,290
- Against the window.
- In the passenger car?

111
00:11:07,084 --> 00:11:08,836
Yeah.

112
00:11:11,463 --> 00:11:13,090
Where are the other passengers?

113
00:11:13,173 --> 00:11:15,217
Was your family traveling with you?

114
00:11:17,803 --> 00:11:19,680
No.

115
00:11:19,763 --> 00:11:22,474
Did you get up from your seat?

116
00:11:28,146 --> 00:11:31,149
Are you certain
you were in the passenger car?

117
00:11:36,488 --> 00:11:37,656
Yes.

118
00:11:46,039 --> 00:11:48,876
Why are you looking at me
like that?

119
00:11:48,959 --> 00:11:51,086
Your train derailed.

120
00:11:51,169 --> 00:11:53,755
Some kind of malfunction.

121
00:11:53,839 --> 00:11:56,925
They only found
two people alive so far.

122
00:11:57,009 --> 00:11:59,261
You and this man.

123
00:12:01,138 --> 00:12:02,639
<i>His skull was cracked open,</i>

124
00:12:02,723 --> 00:12:06,268
<i>and most of his left side
was crushed.</i>

125
00:12:06,685 --> 00:12:09,271
<i>And to answer your question,
there are two reasons</i>

126
00:12:09,354 --> 00:12:12,065
<i>why I'm looking at you like this.</i>

127
00:12:12,149 --> 00:12:14,651
<i>One, because it seems,
in a few minutes,</i>

128
00:12:15,110 --> 00:12:17,988
<i>that you will officially be
the only survivor of this train wreck.</i>

129
00:12:19,448 --> 00:12:22,326
<i>- And two...</i>
- (MONITOR BEEPING RAPIDLY)

130
00:12:22,409 --> 00:12:24,912
<i>...because you didn't
break one bone.</i>

131
00:12:25,954 --> 00:12:28,290
<i>You don't have a scratch on you.</i>

132
00:14:14,021 --> 00:14:15,105
David?

133
00:14:18,483 --> 00:14:20,152
What happened...

134
00:14:20,986 --> 00:14:22,612
...in New York?

135
00:14:24,364 --> 00:14:26,908
I don't think I got the job.

136
00:14:28,577 --> 00:14:30,328
I'm still going...

137
00:14:31,872 --> 00:14:33,665
I'm still moving there.

138
00:14:35,042 --> 00:14:36,668
Just...

139
00:14:37,544 --> 00:14:39,838
Oh, just... just not now.

140
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:47,137
Good night.

141
00:14:50,015 --> 00:14:51,975
Good night.

142
00:14:55,353 --> 00:14:58,523
(CHURCH BELLS TOLLING)

143
00:15:03,987 --> 00:15:09,534
Sara Elaston, social worker
at Broad and Locust Community Center.

144
00:15:10,744 --> 00:15:13,163
We pray for your soul.

145
00:15:15,040 --> 00:15:19,753
Kevin Elliot, businessman,
father of six.

146
00:15:21,379 --> 00:15:23,173
We pray for your soul.

147
00:15:24,841 --> 00:15:27,052
Glen Stevens,

148
00:15:27,135 --> 00:15:30,597
researcher in the area of leukemia

149
00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:32,766
at Drexel University.

150
00:15:34,351 --> 00:15:35,977
We pray for your soul.

151
00:15:38,146 --> 00:15:40,398
Jennifer Pennyman,

152
00:15:40,482 --> 00:15:44,694
third-grade teacher
at Jefferson Elementary.

153
00:15:46,738 --> 00:15:48,824
(TRAILING OFF) <i>We pray for your soul.</i>

154
00:15:54,496 --> 00:15:56,289
(CHURCH BELLS)

155
00:16:33,869 --> 00:16:36,204
(MEN CHATTERING, LAUGHING)

156
00:16:55,807 --> 00:16:57,100
Yes?

157
00:16:57,184 --> 00:16:59,060
Is Noel in, ma'am?

158
00:16:59,144 --> 00:17:01,271
No, sir, he is not.

159
00:17:02,731 --> 00:17:05,233
I read about you in the paper.

160
00:17:05,317 --> 00:17:06,526
What?

161
00:17:06,610 --> 00:17:08,695
I was in an accident once too.

162
00:17:08,778 --> 00:17:11,656
A horse almost
trampled me to death.

163
00:17:11,740 --> 00:17:14,242
- Wow.
- Had him put down.

164
00:17:16,494 --> 00:17:18,038
That's a sad story.

165
00:17:23,126 --> 00:17:25,420
Could you ask Noel
something for me, please?

166
00:17:26,338 --> 00:17:28,798
Proceed.

167
00:17:28,882 --> 00:17:31,176
Ask him to check
how many sick days I've taken

168
00:17:31,259 --> 00:17:35,180
- since I've worked here?
- That the entire message?

169
00:17:35,263 --> 00:17:36,473
Yes, ma'am.

170
00:17:39,809 --> 00:17:41,228
Thank you.

171
00:17:45,357 --> 00:17:48,360
- (RAIN FALLING)
- (PEOPLE SHOUTING)

172
00:17:50,779 --> 00:17:52,697
(THUNDER RUMBLING)

173
00:17:52,781 --> 00:17:55,951
(QUARTERBACK CALLING OUT)

174
00:17:59,412 --> 00:18:00,705
(WHISTLE BLOWS)

175
00:18:19,891 --> 00:18:21,768
Hey, Noel.

176
00:18:21,893 --> 00:18:24,396
What, you hit your head on that train?

177
00:18:24,479 --> 00:18:26,898
Get your brain to start working again?

178
00:18:27,524 --> 00:18:29,192
What?

179
00:18:29,401 --> 00:18:30,986
Forty dollars.

180
00:18:31,987 --> 00:18:34,906
- Forty dollars what?
- You're getting a $40-a-week raise.

181
00:18:34,990 --> 00:18:36,408
That's it.

182
00:18:37,784 --> 00:18:39,369
I checked. You were right.

183
00:18:39,452 --> 00:18:41,413
You've never taken a sick day.

184
00:18:41,496 --> 00:18:44,207
Five years, no sick day.
I get it. You want a raise.

185
00:18:45,125 --> 00:18:47,335
Smart way to make your point.

186
00:19:06,021 --> 00:19:07,022
(KNOCK ON DOOR)

187
00:19:13,987 --> 00:19:15,238
Is Joseph OK?

188
00:19:15,322 --> 00:19:18,408
Yeah. He's asleep.

189
00:19:18,491 --> 00:19:19,659
Oh.

190
00:19:20,827 --> 00:19:22,078
Uh...

191
00:19:23,204 --> 00:19:24,664
I wanted to ask you a question.

192
00:19:26,041 --> 00:19:27,959
It's gonna sound a little strange,

193
00:19:28,043 --> 00:19:31,004
but just think about it
for a second, OK?

194
00:19:31,796 --> 00:19:32,881
OK.

195
00:19:34,507 --> 00:19:36,676
When's the last time I was sick?

196
00:19:36,760 --> 00:19:38,053
Do you remember?

197
00:19:42,223 --> 00:19:44,267
Um, I don't know.

198
00:19:46,311 --> 00:19:47,937
It's been a while.

199
00:19:49,105 --> 00:19:52,650
I haven't been sick this year,
I know that.

200
00:19:52,734 --> 00:19:54,944
OK.

201
00:19:55,028 --> 00:19:57,197
Do you remember me getting sick?

202
00:20:00,450 --> 00:20:01,951
Um...

203
00:20:02,744 --> 00:20:05,080
Not a specific day.

204
00:20:05,163 --> 00:20:07,499
What... what's this about?

205
00:20:09,667 --> 00:20:13,171
Audrey, do you remember me
ever getting sick?

206
00:20:13,254 --> 00:20:15,423
In the three years
we lived in this house?

207
00:20:16,383 --> 00:20:18,385
In the old apartment?

208
00:20:18,468 --> 00:20:21,638
Before Joseph was born?
Before we ever got married?

209
00:20:25,809 --> 00:20:27,811
I... I can't remember.

210
00:20:29,729 --> 00:20:31,898
Don't you think that's kind of weird,

211
00:20:31,981 --> 00:20:36,236
not remembering one cold
or a fever or a sore throat?

212
00:20:38,405 --> 00:20:40,407
What do you think it means?

213
00:20:45,245 --> 00:20:46,663
Um...

214
00:20:48,206 --> 00:20:52,252
I think it means probably
too tired to remember.

215
00:21:18,027 --> 00:21:20,363
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

216
00:21:33,543 --> 00:21:35,420
No more sitting in this room.

217
00:21:35,503 --> 00:21:38,006
I let it go on long enough.

218
00:21:38,089 --> 00:21:41,426
I'm not going out there anymore.
I'm not getting hurt again.

219
00:21:41,509 --> 00:21:45,388
- This was the last time, I told you.
- You can't do anything about that.

220
00:21:45,472 --> 00:21:48,266
You might fall between
this chair and that television.

221
00:21:48,349 --> 00:21:51,394
If that's what God has planned
for you, that's gonna happen.

222
00:21:51,478 --> 00:21:54,189
You can't hide from it
sitting in a room.

223
00:21:57,400 --> 00:21:59,527
They call me Mr. Glass at school

224
00:21:59,611 --> 00:22:01,738
because I break like glass.

225
00:22:06,493 --> 00:22:08,786
You make this decision now...

226
00:22:10,038 --> 00:22:12,165
...to be afraid...

227
00:22:13,666 --> 00:22:15,919
...and you will never turn back.

228
00:22:17,670 --> 00:22:22,550
Your whole life,
you will always be afraid.

229
00:22:37,315 --> 00:22:39,943
- I got a present for you.
- Why?

230
00:22:41,402 --> 00:22:42,820
Forget why.

231
00:22:45,949 --> 00:22:47,617
You want it or not?

232
00:22:50,912 --> 00:22:53,206
Well, go get it, then.

233
00:22:53,289 --> 00:22:54,582
Where is it?

234
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:57,627
On a bench...

235
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:00,547
...across the street.

236
00:23:05,468 --> 00:23:07,804
(CHILDREN CHATTERING)

237
00:23:15,019 --> 00:23:16,771
Someone's gonna take it.

238
00:23:19,023 --> 00:23:21,943
Then you better
get out there soon.

239
00:23:22,026 --> 00:23:24,487
(LAUGHING, SHOUTING)

240
00:24:36,142 --> 00:24:38,019
I bought a whole bunch.

241
00:24:42,607 --> 00:24:44,817
There'll be one of these
waiting for you

242
00:24:44,901 --> 00:24:47,403
every time you want
to come out here.

243
00:24:53,701 --> 00:24:56,746
They say this one
has a surprise ending.

244
00:25:05,880 --> 00:25:08,466
This is from Fritz Campion's
own library.

245
00:25:08,549 --> 00:25:13,137
This is before the first issue of
the comic book hit the stands in 1968.

246
00:25:13,888 --> 00:25:18,101
It's a classic depiction
of good versus evil.

247
00:25:18,184 --> 00:25:20,019
Notice the square jaw of Slayer,

248
00:25:20,103 --> 00:25:21,979
common in most comic heroes.

249
00:25:22,063 --> 00:25:25,858
And the slightly disproportionate size
of Jaguaro's head to his body.

250
00:25:25,942 --> 00:25:29,028
This again is common,
but only in villains.

251
00:25:29,112 --> 00:25:30,238
Mmm.

252
00:25:30,321 --> 00:25:32,532
The thing to notice about this piece,

253
00:25:32,615 --> 00:25:36,744
the thing that makes it
very, very special

254
00:25:36,828 --> 00:25:39,831
is its realistic depiction
of its figures.

255
00:25:39,914 --> 00:25:42,041
When the characters
reached the magazine,

256
00:25:42,125 --> 00:25:44,210
they were exaggerated...

257
00:25:44,293 --> 00:25:46,629
...as always happens.

258
00:25:46,713 --> 00:25:50,508
This... is vintage.

259
00:25:53,010 --> 00:25:54,220
Well...

260
00:26:00,226 --> 00:26:02,019
...wrap it up.

261
00:26:02,937 --> 00:26:05,523
You've made
a considerably wise decision.

262
00:26:10,862 --> 00:26:12,864
Well, my kid's gonna go berserk.

263
00:26:16,075 --> 00:26:17,910
Once again, please?

264
00:26:17,994 --> 00:26:20,955
My son Jeb, it's a gift for him.

265
00:26:21,038 --> 00:26:24,083
- How old is Jeb?
- He's four.

266
00:26:27,044 --> 00:26:29,130
No.

267
00:26:29,213 --> 00:26:33,217
No, no, no, no, no.

268
00:26:34,302 --> 00:26:37,263
You need to go. Now.

269
00:26:37,346 --> 00:26:38,514
(SCOFFS)

270
00:26:38,598 --> 00:26:40,808
What... what'd I say?

271
00:26:40,892 --> 00:26:43,394
Do you see any Teletubbies in here?

272
00:26:43,978 --> 00:26:45,813
Do you see
a slender plastic tag

273
00:26:45,897 --> 00:26:48,566
clipped to my shirt
with my name printed on it?

274
00:26:48,649 --> 00:26:51,611
An Asian child
with a blank expression sitting outside

275
00:26:51,694 --> 00:26:55,364
in a mechanical helicopter that shakes
when you put quarters in it?

276
00:26:55,448 --> 00:26:56,991
No?

277
00:26:57,074 --> 00:26:58,951
Well, that's what you see
at a toy store,

278
00:26:59,035 --> 00:27:01,245
and you must think
you're in a toy store

279
00:27:01,370 --> 00:27:04,165
because you're here shopping
for an infant named Jeb.

280
00:27:05,124 --> 00:27:08,127
Now one of us
has made a gross error

281
00:27:08,211 --> 00:27:10,963
and wasted the other person's
valuable time.

282
00:27:11,380 --> 00:27:13,424
This is an art gallery, my friend,

283
00:27:13,508 --> 00:27:14,592
and this...

284
00:27:15,009 --> 00:27:16,969
...is a piece of art.

285
00:27:32,568 --> 00:27:34,237
We're by appointment only.

286
00:27:36,280 --> 00:27:40,117
- I got a card from this store.
- Congratulations, you have a mailbox.

287
00:27:40,201 --> 00:27:42,745
The exhibition isn't for two weeks.

288
00:27:42,829 --> 00:27:46,165
This one was under
the windshield wiper of my car.

289
00:27:48,876 --> 00:27:51,629
How certain are you
that you've never taken ill?

290
00:27:51,712 --> 00:27:54,215
Seventy-five percent.

291
00:27:54,298 --> 00:27:56,050
Well...

292
00:27:56,133 --> 00:27:59,011
...that's not certain at all, is it?

293
00:27:59,262 --> 00:28:01,681
That's hole number one.

294
00:28:02,098 --> 00:28:05,309
I'm going to be extremely
skeptical about all this.

295
00:28:05,393 --> 00:28:06,936
DAVID: About all what?

296
00:28:09,105 --> 00:28:12,191
I'm assuming you've never been injured.
Would I be wrong in that assumption?

297
00:28:12,275 --> 00:28:14,193
JOSEPH: Dad's been injured.

298
00:28:15,570 --> 00:28:17,280
Is the child correct?

299
00:28:18,614 --> 00:28:20,533
Yes, sir.

300
00:28:20,616 --> 00:28:22,869
In college, I was in a car accident.

301
00:28:22,952 --> 00:28:26,372
- Was it serious?
- He couldn't play football anymore.

302
00:28:27,707 --> 00:28:29,292
That's hole number two.

303
00:28:31,669 --> 00:28:33,713
That's a big one.

304
00:28:37,133 --> 00:28:41,012
Mr. Price, can we talk about the note
that you left on my car?

305
00:28:41,095 --> 00:28:44,098
I've studied the form
of comics intimately.

306
00:28:44,181 --> 00:28:46,684
I've spent a third of my life
in a hospital bed

307
00:28:46,767 --> 00:28:48,895
with nothing else to do but read.

308
00:28:49,937 --> 00:28:52,064
I believe comics are our last link

309
00:28:52,148 --> 00:28:55,693
to an ancient way
of passing on history.

310
00:28:55,776 --> 00:28:57,904
The Egyptians drew on the walls.

311
00:28:57,987 --> 00:29:02,158
Countries all over the world still pass
on knowledge through pictorial forms.

312
00:29:02,241 --> 00:29:04,702
I believe comics
are a form of history

313
00:29:05,286 --> 00:29:07,997
that someone somewhere
felt or experienced.

314
00:29:08,539 --> 00:29:11,334
Then, of course, those experiences
and history got chewed up

315
00:29:11,417 --> 00:29:14,962
in the commercial machine,
got jazzed up, made titillating,

316
00:29:15,046 --> 00:29:16,797
cartooned for the sale rack.

317
00:29:18,299 --> 00:29:21,510
This city has seen its share
of disasters.

318
00:29:21,594 --> 00:29:24,221
I watched the aftermath
of that plane crash.

319
00:29:24,305 --> 00:29:26,933
I watched the carnage
of the hotel fire.

320
00:29:27,016 --> 00:29:28,935
I watched the news waiting to hear

321
00:29:29,018 --> 00:29:34,273
a very specific combination of words,
but they never came.

322
00:29:34,357 --> 00:29:38,402
Then one day I saw a news story
about a train accident,

323
00:29:38,527 --> 00:29:40,404
and I heard them.

324
00:29:40,488 --> 00:29:42,531
"There is a sole survivor,

325
00:29:42,615 --> 00:29:45,952
and he is miraculously unharmed."

326
00:29:48,621 --> 00:29:52,249
I have something called
osteogenesis imperfecta.

327
00:29:52,333 --> 00:29:56,420
It's a genetic disorder. I don't make
a particular protein very well,

328
00:29:56,504 --> 00:29:59,256
and it makes my bones
very low in density.

329
00:29:59,340 --> 00:30:01,384
Very easy to break.

330
00:30:01,467 --> 00:30:04,220
I've had 54 breaks in my life,

331
00:30:04,303 --> 00:30:09,058
and I have the tamest version
of this disorder, type one.

332
00:30:09,141 --> 00:30:12,603
There are type two,
type three, type four.

333
00:30:12,687 --> 00:30:16,273
Type fours don't last very long.

334
00:30:16,357 --> 00:30:19,527
So that's how it popped into my head.

335
00:30:19,986 --> 00:30:22,321
If there is someone like me
in the world,

336
00:30:22,405 --> 00:30:24,573
and I'm at one end of the spectrum,

337
00:30:24,657 --> 00:30:29,203
couldn't there be someone else
the opposite of me at the other end?

338
00:30:29,286 --> 00:30:30,871
Someone who doesn't get sick,

339
00:30:30,955 --> 00:30:33,624
who doesn't get hurt
like the rest of us?

340
00:30:33,708 --> 00:30:37,253
And he probably
doesn't even know it.

341
00:30:37,795 --> 00:30:41,716
The kind of person
these stories are about.

342
00:30:42,717 --> 00:30:47,888
A person put here
to protect the rest of us.

343
00:30:47,972 --> 00:30:49,390
To guard us.

344
00:30:52,143 --> 00:30:53,686
You think my dad's a real...

345
00:30:53,769 --> 00:30:57,231
I don't think anything right now.

346
00:30:57,314 --> 00:30:59,442
It's a possibility...

347
00:30:59,775 --> 00:31:02,028
...one with many holes.

348
00:31:05,489 --> 00:31:08,409
Joseph, don't take another sip
of that water.

349
00:31:11,203 --> 00:31:12,621
Go throw it in the trash.

350
00:31:12,705 --> 00:31:15,666
- But, Dad...
- Do it now, please.

351
00:31:23,466 --> 00:31:24,925
(DOOR OPENS)

352
00:31:25,009 --> 00:31:27,678
I see guys like you
all the time in my work.

353
00:31:27,762 --> 00:31:28,804
(DOOR CLOSES)

354
00:31:28,888 --> 00:31:32,224
You find someone
you want to take advantage of,

355
00:31:32,308 --> 00:31:35,728
you tell them a fantastic story,

356
00:31:35,811 --> 00:31:38,731
and somewhere in there
you say it:

357
00:31:38,814 --> 00:31:41,650
"I just need
your credit card number...

358
00:31:41,734 --> 00:31:44,528
...a small down payment."

359
00:31:46,489 --> 00:31:48,866
This morning was the first morning
I could remember

360
00:31:48,949 --> 00:31:51,452
that I didn't open my eyes
and feel...

361
00:31:53,245 --> 00:31:55,206
...sadness.

362
00:31:59,168 --> 00:32:02,338
I thought the person who wrote
that note had an answer for me.

363
00:32:02,421 --> 00:32:04,256
(DOOR OPENS)

364
00:32:04,340 --> 00:32:06,509
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACH)

365
00:32:09,428 --> 00:32:10,763
I'm gonna leave now.

366
00:32:12,556 --> 00:32:14,433
Good luck with your exhibition.

367
00:32:14,517 --> 00:32:16,685
So, what kind of job
do you have, David?

368
00:32:16,769 --> 00:32:19,355
You said you've met guys like me
in your work.

369
00:32:19,438 --> 00:32:21,148
What exactly is it that you do?

370
00:32:21,232 --> 00:32:25,569
I work at the university stadium.
I'm a security guard.

371
00:32:56,767 --> 00:32:58,185
(DOOR OPENS)

372
00:34:29,610 --> 00:34:30,611
(KNOCK ON DOOR)

373
00:34:35,908 --> 00:34:37,576
(KNOCKING CONTINUES)

374
00:34:41,830 --> 00:34:43,123
Hi.

375
00:34:44,416 --> 00:34:46,043
I've come to a decision.

376
00:34:47,586 --> 00:34:48,921
OK.

377
00:34:49,755 --> 00:34:52,466
I just want to ask you
something, OK?

378
00:34:52,549 --> 00:34:55,761
And you can be totally honest.

379
00:34:55,844 --> 00:35:00,349
I'm prepared for any answer,
and it won't affect me.

380
00:35:02,476 --> 00:35:05,062
Have you been with anyone?

381
00:35:05,145 --> 00:35:07,481
I mean, since we've been
having problems.

382
00:35:07,564 --> 00:35:09,066
The answer won't affect me.

383
00:35:12,528 --> 00:35:15,447
I just need to know, you know?

384
00:35:15,531 --> 00:35:18,075
It won't affect me either way.

385
00:35:19,868 --> 00:35:21,203
No.

386
00:35:27,042 --> 00:35:28,544
(INHALES SHARPLY)

387
00:35:29,753 --> 00:35:32,256
(SOBBING) Sorry, David. Sorry.

388
00:35:37,303 --> 00:35:38,804
Anyway...

389
00:35:39,555 --> 00:35:41,515
...my decision is...

390
00:35:43,559 --> 00:35:44,685
(SNIFFLES)

391
00:35:44,768 --> 00:35:46,687
...that I want to start again.

392
00:35:50,357 --> 00:35:54,069
That I want it to be like it was
in the beginning.

393
00:35:56,488 --> 00:36:00,117
And it's a big deal that you
walked away from that train.

394
00:36:02,870 --> 00:36:05,205
I feel like it's a second chance.

395
00:36:11,086 --> 00:36:14,631
And if you feel like
asking me out sometime,

396
00:36:14,715 --> 00:36:16,216
it'd be OK.

397
00:36:18,886 --> 00:36:20,304
OK.

398
00:36:28,854 --> 00:36:32,274
- (MARCHING BAND PLAYING)
- (MAN SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY ON PA)

399
00:36:33,442 --> 00:36:35,694
- MAN: (ON WALKIE-TALKIE) <i>Dunn.</i>
- Go for Dunn.

400
00:36:35,778 --> 00:36:37,404
<i>It's Jenkins.</i>

401
00:36:37,488 --> 00:36:40,616
<i>Look, we got a guy over
at gate 17-C with a bogus ticket.</i>

402
00:36:40,699 --> 00:36:43,410
<i>Says he knows you.
He won't tell me his name.</i>

403
00:36:44,745 --> 00:36:47,081
What's he look like?

404
00:36:47,164 --> 00:36:48,791
Why is it, do you think,

405
00:36:48,874 --> 00:36:51,710
that, of all the professions
in the world, you chose protection?

406
00:36:53,796 --> 00:36:55,422
You are a very strange man.

407
00:36:55,506 --> 00:36:58,342
You could have been a tax accountant,
owned your own gym,

408
00:36:58,425 --> 00:37:02,179
opened a chain of restaurants.
You could've done one of 10,000 things,

409
00:37:02,262 --> 00:37:06,392
but in the end,
you chose to protect people.

410
00:37:06,475 --> 00:37:08,227
You made that decision,

411
00:37:08,310 --> 00:37:11,105
and I find that
very, very interesting.

412
00:37:12,356 --> 00:37:15,275
Now all I need is
your credit card number.

413
00:37:16,360 --> 00:37:18,278
That last part was a joke.

414
00:37:19,738 --> 00:37:21,156
(DAVID CHUCKLES)

415
00:37:22,491 --> 00:37:24,535
You're a big sports fan, are you?

416
00:37:24,618 --> 00:37:27,162
It's starting to intrigue me.

417
00:37:28,414 --> 00:37:30,999
It gets heaviest
ten minutes before kickoff.

418
00:37:43,262 --> 00:37:45,264
MAN: Program here. Program.

419
00:37:45,347 --> 00:37:46,682
Program.

420
00:37:49,309 --> 00:37:50,394
Program.

421
00:37:52,855 --> 00:37:55,065
- Rich, why don't we pat down?
- Right.

422
00:37:55,149 --> 00:37:57,192
- Just give me a minute.
- Is there a problem?

423
00:37:57,317 --> 00:37:59,486
I'm sorry, I'm gonna
have to search you.

424
00:38:00,362 --> 00:38:02,865
The tall guy in
the camouflage jacket.

425
00:38:02,990 --> 00:38:05,576
Sometimes people
carry weapons in here,

426
00:38:05,659 --> 00:38:07,578
and they drink too much.

427
00:38:07,661 --> 00:38:11,248
If the team's not doing good,
bad things can happen.

428
00:38:11,999 --> 00:38:15,627
We do pat-downs of the crowd
to discourage people from carrying.

429
00:38:16,753 --> 00:38:19,089
If he's carrying,
he'll step out of line.

430
00:38:32,769 --> 00:38:34,313
Yeah, step right up here.

431
00:38:35,230 --> 00:38:36,899
(COUGHS)

432
00:38:43,447 --> 00:38:44,615
DAVID: OK.

433
00:38:45,866 --> 00:38:48,327
I got you a seat
up in the yellow section.

434
00:38:48,410 --> 00:38:49,745
It's in the nosebleeds,

435
00:38:49,828 --> 00:38:52,080
but at least you won't get spit on.

436
00:38:53,373 --> 00:38:54,541
All right.

437
00:38:54,625 --> 00:38:57,961
How'd you know that guy you bumped
was carrying a weapon?

438
00:39:00,005 --> 00:39:02,299
I don't know.

439
00:39:02,382 --> 00:39:06,803
Maybe it was that camouflage
army jacket he was wearing.

440
00:39:06,887 --> 00:39:10,182
Those guys like to carry
hunting knives and stuff for show.

441
00:39:10,265 --> 00:39:12,226
You thought
he was carrying a knife?

442
00:39:13,185 --> 00:39:15,229
I thought he was
carrying something, yeah.

443
00:39:15,312 --> 00:39:16,772
But not a knife.

444
00:39:19,858 --> 00:39:21,818
I got a...

445
00:39:21,902 --> 00:39:24,905
...picture of a silver gun
with a black grip

446
00:39:24,988 --> 00:39:27,324
tucked in his pants.

447
00:39:27,407 --> 00:39:29,952
You know, like on TV.

448
00:39:31,203 --> 00:39:33,956
You have good instincts
for things like that?

449
00:39:34,039 --> 00:39:35,207
Like what?

450
00:39:35,290 --> 00:39:38,460
Knowing when people
have done something wrong.

451
00:39:40,879 --> 00:39:42,214
Yeah.

452
00:39:42,297 --> 00:39:45,133
Have you ever tried to develop it?

453
00:39:45,926 --> 00:39:47,553
I don't know what you're asking me.

454
00:39:47,636 --> 00:39:48,887
Your skill.

455
00:39:50,222 --> 00:39:52,641
Look, I got to be down
on the sidelines during the game.

456
00:39:52,724 --> 00:39:54,309
You can get to your seat
right down...

457
00:39:54,393 --> 00:39:57,646
Characters in comics are often
attributed special powers...

458
00:39:57,729 --> 00:40:01,858
Invisibility, X-ray vision,
things of that sort.

459
00:40:01,942 --> 00:40:03,735
OK.

460
00:40:04,987 --> 00:40:06,613
I don't want to play
this game anymore.

461
00:40:06,697 --> 00:40:09,157
It's an exaggeration of the truth.

462
00:40:09,449 --> 00:40:13,412
Maybe it's based on something
as simple as instinct.

463
00:40:14,746 --> 00:40:16,748
But he might not have been
carrying anything.

464
00:40:16,832 --> 00:40:18,333
But he might have been
carrying a silver gun

465
00:40:18,417 --> 00:40:20,377
with a black grip tucked in his pants.

466
00:40:20,460 --> 00:40:22,296
MAN: (ON WALKIE-TALKIE) <i>Dunn.</i>

467
00:40:22,379 --> 00:40:23,714
I gotta go.

468
00:40:25,799 --> 00:40:28,010
Yeah, I know. I'll be right down.

469
00:40:28,135 --> 00:40:31,263
- One last question.
- What?

470
00:40:31,346 --> 00:40:35,434
That car accident you were in.
Was there anyone else involved?

471
00:40:37,853 --> 00:40:41,273
Yeah. My wife Audrey.

472
00:40:41,356 --> 00:40:43,692
She was in the car with me.

473
00:40:45,611 --> 00:40:47,738
Have a good life, Elijah.

474
00:40:48,322 --> 00:40:51,908
Next time, try to buy your tickets
at an authorized sales location.

475
00:40:51,992 --> 00:40:54,786
(CHATTER ON WALKIE-TALKIE)

476
00:40:54,870 --> 00:40:56,121
I'm on my way.

477
00:40:58,874 --> 00:40:59,958
(ENGINE TURNS OVER)

478
00:41:00,042 --> 00:41:02,961
MAN: (ON RADIO)
<i>...into the investigation of train 177</i>

479
00:41:03,045 --> 00:41:05,797
<i>being conducted at the Eastrail
holding warehouses.</i>

480
00:41:05,881 --> 00:41:08,342
<i>The city council has stepped up
plans to pass a bill</i>

481
00:41:08,425 --> 00:41:11,887
<i>requiring higher standards
for inspection and maintenance</i>

482
00:41:11,970 --> 00:41:16,058
<i>of all rail transportation in
Philadelphia and its outlying districts.</i>

483
00:41:24,232 --> 00:41:26,026
Hold up a second!

484
00:42:21,039 --> 00:42:23,709
I just want to ask you something.

485
00:42:51,737 --> 00:42:55,365
- (BONES CRACKING)
- (ELIJAH SCREAMING)

486
00:42:58,160 --> 00:42:59,911
(CRYING)

487
00:43:02,998 --> 00:43:04,666
(GASPING)

488
00:43:33,320 --> 00:43:34,905
JOSEPH: Dad!

489
00:43:34,988 --> 00:43:36,156
Hey, Dad!

490
00:43:43,079 --> 00:43:44,164
Dad!

491
00:43:55,550 --> 00:43:57,594
Joseph, do you know how mad
your mother would be

492
00:43:57,677 --> 00:44:01,264
- if she knew you were playing football?
- Are you gonna tell?

493
00:44:02,766 --> 00:44:06,061
You want to play last set of downs?
We got a big guy like you.

494
00:44:06,144 --> 00:44:08,605
You could play on opposite sides.

495
00:44:08,688 --> 00:44:10,232
He's Potter's cousin.

496
00:44:10,607 --> 00:44:13,026
He's starting cornerback
at Temple University.

497
00:44:13,109 --> 00:44:15,195
He's going pro in the draft.

498
00:44:15,278 --> 00:44:18,698
They say he can run
the 40 in 4.3 seconds.

499
00:44:18,782 --> 00:44:20,700
I've heard.

500
00:44:20,784 --> 00:44:23,829
You can beat him, Dad.
Let's beat 'em.

501
00:44:23,912 --> 00:44:25,080
I'm gonna go in.

502
00:44:25,163 --> 00:44:28,041
Just one set of downs.
I told them you were great.

503
00:44:28,124 --> 00:44:29,626
- Why'd you do that?
- Just one.

504
00:44:30,126 --> 00:44:32,462
I'm gonna go in.
I've got some things to do.

505
00:44:32,546 --> 00:44:35,090
- What things?
- I'm gonna work out.

506
00:44:35,382 --> 00:44:37,217
- I'll help you.
- I don't need...

507
00:44:37,300 --> 00:44:40,303
I can't play, guys!
I'm working out with my dad!

508
00:44:44,099 --> 00:44:46,017
Hut! You're doing good.

509
00:44:46,101 --> 00:44:47,227
Right there! Catch!

510
00:44:47,310 --> 00:44:51,565
(GREGG ALLMAN: <i>MIDNIGHT RIDER)</i>

511
00:45:01,950 --> 00:45:03,410
How much did you put on there?

512
00:45:13,169 --> 00:45:15,839
You put too much.
That's 250 pounds.

513
00:45:17,007 --> 00:45:19,301
How much can you lift?

514
00:45:21,052 --> 00:45:23,054
That's the most I ever lifted.

515
00:45:25,307 --> 00:45:27,517
That could've been dangerous, Joseph.

516
00:45:28,393 --> 00:45:30,729
Why don't you go upstairs now
and let me finish up, OK?

517
00:45:30,812 --> 00:45:32,898
I'll take it off.
I'll help you right.

518
00:45:38,028 --> 00:45:41,615
You think you could have
beaten up Bruce Lee?

519
00:45:41,698 --> 00:45:43,241
No.

520
00:45:43,742 --> 00:45:47,370
- I mean, if you knew karate.
- No.

521
00:45:52,208 --> 00:45:55,629
What if he wasn't allowed to kick,
and you were really mad at him?

522
00:45:55,712 --> 00:45:57,255
No, Joseph.

523
00:46:27,953 --> 00:46:29,204
(GRUNTS)

524
00:46:38,713 --> 00:46:40,632
How much did you take off?

525
00:46:43,510 --> 00:46:45,095
I lied.

526
00:46:48,640 --> 00:46:50,016
You added?

527
00:46:58,274 --> 00:47:00,235
How much is it?

528
00:47:01,945 --> 00:47:04,155
Two hundred and seventy pounds.

529
00:47:07,409 --> 00:47:08,785
Let's put more.

530
00:47:12,455 --> 00:47:14,332
OK.

531
00:47:18,169 --> 00:47:20,755
Why don't you move back a little
just to be safe, OK?

532
00:47:26,344 --> 00:47:28,263
(GRUNTS, EXHALES)

533
00:47:53,830 --> 00:47:55,081
More?

534
00:48:07,093 --> 00:48:09,471
You should never do
anything like this.

535
00:48:09,554 --> 00:48:11,097
You know that, right?

536
00:48:12,640 --> 00:48:15,310
What do you do
if something bad happens?

537
00:48:15,393 --> 00:48:16,644
Get Mom.

538
00:48:18,063 --> 00:48:19,898
Right.

539
00:48:54,849 --> 00:48:56,893
How much did you put on that time?

540
00:49:01,397 --> 00:49:03,650
All of it.

541
00:49:05,485 --> 00:49:07,237
What else can we use?

542
00:49:11,533 --> 00:49:13,368
(DAVID GRUNTING)

543
00:49:33,930 --> 00:49:35,515
How much is it?

544
00:49:44,774 --> 00:49:46,609
How much is it, Dad?

545
00:49:49,779 --> 00:49:52,031
About 350 pounds.

546
00:49:59,455 --> 00:50:01,749
DOCTOR:
Fracture of the fifth metacarpal,

547
00:50:01,833 --> 00:50:05,170
as well as multiple fractures of
the sixth, seventh and eighth ribs.

548
00:50:05,295 --> 00:50:06,421
The worst of the injury

549
00:50:06,504 --> 00:50:09,883
was sustained to the right leg
in the form of a spiral fracture.

550
00:50:09,966 --> 00:50:13,052
There were 14 breaks.
It simply shattered.

551
00:50:14,679 --> 00:50:17,098
They call me Mr. Glass.

552
00:50:17,557 --> 00:50:19,851
- DOCTOR: Who does?
- Kids.

553
00:50:21,186 --> 00:50:24,564
DOCTOR: Are you all right, Mr. Price?
Shall I continue?

554
00:50:27,025 --> 00:50:29,444
Pins were placed throughout
the length of the leg.

555
00:50:29,527 --> 00:50:32,697
The use of a wheelchair will be
needed for a two-month period.

556
00:50:32,780 --> 00:50:35,658
The use of crutches will follow
for 12 to 14 months.

557
00:50:35,742 --> 00:50:38,119
Hospital stay will range
from five to eight days

558
00:50:38,203 --> 00:50:40,955
followed by nine to 12 months
of physical therapy.

559
00:50:41,039 --> 00:50:43,124
Prescribed medication
for pain management

560
00:50:43,208 --> 00:50:47,128
<i>will take the usual forms
of morphine drip, Percocet, Darvocet.</i>

561
00:50:58,056 --> 00:51:02,518
Your 10:00 is here. The hospital
discharged him this morning.

562
00:51:04,562 --> 00:51:05,772
Thanks.

563
00:51:14,781 --> 00:51:16,950
Elijah, right?

564
00:51:17,033 --> 00:51:19,452
We're gonna prevent
any substantial atrophy

565
00:51:19,535 --> 00:51:22,914
of your good leg with this,
and it works your quadriceps.

566
00:51:24,040 --> 00:51:25,833
How long have you been married?

567
00:51:28,836 --> 00:51:30,213
Twelve years.

568
00:51:30,296 --> 00:51:32,423
How'd you get together?

569
00:51:32,507 --> 00:51:34,217
(NERVOUS CHUCKLE)

570
00:51:34,300 --> 00:51:36,177
I'm a little nervous being here.

571
00:51:36,261 --> 00:51:40,014
I ask too many questions
when I'm nervous.

572
00:51:41,683 --> 00:51:43,768
A car accident.

573
00:51:43,851 --> 00:51:45,019
Oh.

574
00:51:45,103 --> 00:51:47,814
Now you're gonna have
to tell me more.

575
00:51:47,897 --> 00:51:49,899
(CHUCKLES SOFTLY)

576
00:51:52,110 --> 00:51:54,320
My husband
was a star athlete in college,

577
00:51:54,404 --> 00:51:56,864
and we were
in an accident together.

578
00:51:57,865 --> 00:52:00,535
Our car flipped on an icy road,
we were both injured.

579
00:52:00,618 --> 00:52:03,413
He couldn't play football anymore.
If that hadn't happened,

580
00:52:03,538 --> 00:52:05,373
we wouldn't have been together.

581
00:52:05,456 --> 00:52:06,874
How so?

582
00:52:06,958 --> 00:52:09,043
I think we should
talk about your rehab.

583
00:52:09,127 --> 00:52:11,421
You don't have to answer
if you don't want to.

584
00:52:12,422 --> 00:52:16,342
So tell me more
about the quad machine.

585
00:52:17,343 --> 00:52:22,932
It prevents atrophy by...
I couldn't spend my life

586
00:52:23,016 --> 00:52:26,060
with someone who played football.
It's pretty much that simple.

587
00:52:27,645 --> 00:52:30,898
I don't hate the game. I admire
the amount of skill it involves.

588
00:52:30,982 --> 00:52:33,943
Like everyone else, I was in awe
of the way he played it,

589
00:52:34,027 --> 00:52:37,488
but football, in many ways,
is the opposite of what I do.

590
00:52:39,198 --> 00:52:41,993
You're rewarded the more
you punish your opponent.

591
00:52:42,076 --> 00:52:44,996
It's too much about violence,
and I don't want violence in my life.

592
00:52:46,539 --> 00:52:50,168
It's not a thing many people
can understand, but...

593
00:52:50,251 --> 00:52:53,296
Anyway, fate stepped in
with that car accident

594
00:52:53,379 --> 00:52:55,673
and took football
out of the equation.

595
00:52:55,757 --> 00:52:58,134
And everyone lived happily ever after.

596
00:53:00,470 --> 00:53:02,055
Sort of.

597
00:53:02,138 --> 00:53:04,974
What part of David's body was injured?

598
00:53:06,851 --> 00:53:08,895
Who said my husband's name
was David?

599
00:53:09,937 --> 00:53:12,190
- (MARCHING BAND PLAYING)
- (CHEERING)

600
00:53:16,235 --> 00:53:19,655
- Let's just go to our seats, OK?
- MAN: (ON PA) <i>Ladies and gentlemen...</i>

601
00:53:19,781 --> 00:53:21,783
(MAN CONTINUES INDISTINCTLY ON PA)

602
00:53:31,250 --> 00:53:33,002
In the first half...

603
00:53:53,815 --> 00:53:56,484
BOY: (SHOUTING, ECHOING) No!

604
00:54:02,407 --> 00:54:04,909
ELIJAH: <i>A 737 crashes on takeoff,</i>

605
00:54:04,992 --> 00:54:07,370
<i>172 die, no survivors.</i>

606
00:54:07,453 --> 00:54:09,539
A hotel fire downtown,

607
00:54:09,622 --> 00:54:12,417
211 die, no survivors.

608
00:54:12,500 --> 00:54:16,587
An Eastrail train derails seven
and a half miles outside the city,

609
00:54:16,671 --> 00:54:18,923
131 die,

610
00:54:19,006 --> 00:54:20,341
one survivor.

611
00:54:20,842 --> 00:54:23,219
He is unharmed.

612
00:54:23,302 --> 00:54:26,264
I've spoken with your husband
about his survival.

613
00:54:26,347 --> 00:54:29,183
I suggested a rather
unbelievable possibility.

614
00:54:29,267 --> 00:54:32,478
Since then, I've come
to believe that possibility,

615
00:54:32,562 --> 00:54:37,024
however unbelievable,
is now more a probability.

616
00:54:37,108 --> 00:54:38,985
And what was it you suggested?

617
00:54:40,111 --> 00:54:42,947
These are mediocre times,
Mrs. Dunn.

618
00:54:43,030 --> 00:54:46,492
People are starting to lose hope.
It's hard for many to believe

619
00:54:46,576 --> 00:54:50,455
there are extraordinary things
inside themselves as well as others.

620
00:54:50,538 --> 00:54:53,749
I hope you can keep
an open mind.

621
00:55:06,762 --> 00:55:08,890
- CROWD: Defense!
- (DRUMBEAT)

622
00:55:08,973 --> 00:55:10,558
- Defense!
- (DRUMBEAT)

623
00:55:10,641 --> 00:55:11,976
- Defense!
- (DRUMBEAT)

624
00:55:12,059 --> 00:55:13,811
- Defense!
- (DRUMBEAT)

625
00:55:13,895 --> 00:55:15,480
- Defense!
- (DRUMBEAT)

626
00:55:30,953 --> 00:55:32,830
MAN: OK, here you go.

627
00:55:32,914 --> 00:55:34,415
Thanks a lot.

628
00:55:35,750 --> 00:55:37,126
Excuse me, sir?

629
00:55:38,461 --> 00:55:40,213
Can you step out of line, please?

630
00:55:44,926 --> 00:55:48,971
We've been having problems with people
selling drugs here in the stadium.

631
00:55:49,055 --> 00:55:51,307
Would you mind
if I check your pockets?

632
00:55:52,391 --> 00:55:54,310
Raise your arms, please.

633
00:55:55,436 --> 00:55:57,480
Come on, raise them up.

634
00:55:57,563 --> 00:55:59,732
I just got here, man.

635
00:56:10,326 --> 00:56:12,036
Can I put my hands down now?

636
00:56:17,959 --> 00:56:19,627
Can I go?

637
00:56:21,254 --> 00:56:22,421
Yeah.

638
00:56:24,882 --> 00:56:27,009
Hope you find them.

639
00:56:35,851 --> 00:56:37,186
MAN: (ON WALKIE-TALKIE) <i>Yo, Dunn.</i>

640
00:56:39,105 --> 00:56:40,481
Yeah.

641
00:56:40,565 --> 00:56:42,900
<i>I just came into the office.
Your kid was hurt.</i>

642
00:56:44,819 --> 00:56:47,822
- Where is he?
<i>- Some play rehearsal at school.</i>

643
00:56:47,905 --> 00:56:49,824
<i>He wants you
to come down there.</i>

644
00:57:04,797 --> 00:57:06,882
Is that your dad?

645
00:57:10,511 --> 00:57:12,597
I bet my dad
could beat up your dad.

646
00:57:16,475 --> 00:57:19,854
No. He...
he insisted we call only you,

647
00:57:19,937 --> 00:57:22,398
though we had some trouble
tracking you down.

648
00:57:22,481 --> 00:57:24,275
I mean, you're not on our list.

649
00:57:25,526 --> 00:57:29,864
- Audrey handles this type of stuff.
- Oh, what type of stuff?

650
00:57:31,490 --> 00:57:33,242
Joseph stuff.

651
00:57:36,370 --> 00:57:39,290
Do I need to put any smelly
ointment on him or anything?

652
00:57:39,373 --> 00:57:41,375
No, no. It's more emotional damage,

653
00:57:41,459 --> 00:57:45,004
not... not too serious, physically.

654
00:57:45,087 --> 00:57:48,424
Nothing like when I sent you
to the hospital.

655
00:57:52,345 --> 00:57:54,639
When was that?

656
00:57:54,722 --> 00:57:59,310
My office was on the other side
of the building back then.

657
00:58:01,937 --> 00:58:04,273
You don't remember me, do you?

658
00:58:06,525 --> 00:58:08,152
No, ma'am.

659
00:58:08,235 --> 00:58:10,237
I had red hair.

660
00:58:12,782 --> 00:58:17,578
Well, you were a little younger
than Joseph when it happened.

661
00:58:17,662 --> 00:58:19,622
Did you know that we changed

662
00:58:19,705 --> 00:58:23,959
the rules of conduct
around the pool because of you?

663
00:58:26,420 --> 00:58:30,966
The kids still tell about it
like it was some sort of ghost story.

664
00:58:32,093 --> 00:58:35,930
"Did you know there was a kid
nearly drowned in that pool?

665
00:58:37,848 --> 00:58:41,310
He lay on the bottom of the pool
for five minutes,

666
00:58:41,394 --> 00:58:45,022
and when they pulled him out,
he was dead."

667
00:58:50,111 --> 00:58:53,406
We let them tell it.
It helps keep them safe.

668
00:58:57,576 --> 00:59:00,121
You still phobic of water?

669
00:59:02,873 --> 00:59:04,917
JOSEPH: <i>It was Potter and another guy.</i>

670
00:59:06,377 --> 00:59:10,339
They were messing with this
Chinese girl in the dressing room.

671
00:59:10,423 --> 00:59:13,926
You can't let bad things happen
to good people, right?

672
00:59:15,010 --> 00:59:16,345
That's your code, right?

673
00:59:16,429 --> 00:59:18,389
That's the hero's code.

674
00:59:20,141 --> 00:59:23,352
I tried to make them stop,
but they kept pushing me down,

675
00:59:23,436 --> 00:59:25,938
and they wouldn't let me get back up.

676
00:59:28,941 --> 00:59:31,402
I thought maybe
'cause you're my dad...

677
00:59:32,987 --> 00:59:34,697
...I thought I might be like you.

678
00:59:38,659 --> 00:59:40,828
I'm not like you.

679
00:59:42,705 --> 00:59:44,623
You are like me.

680
00:59:45,958 --> 00:59:47,585
We can both get hurt.

681
00:59:49,587 --> 00:59:52,840
- I'm just an ordinary man.
- No, you're not.

682
00:59:54,008 --> 00:59:56,093
Why do you keep saying that?

683
01:00:04,268 --> 01:00:07,605
AUDREY: Elijah Price came
to visit me at the center today.

684
01:00:08,606 --> 01:00:09,899
Jesus.

685
01:00:11,650 --> 01:00:14,695
He didn't do anything.
He just...

686
01:00:14,779 --> 01:00:16,739
...told me his theory.

687
01:00:17,823 --> 01:00:20,451
That's sad,
when patients get like that.

688
01:00:20,534 --> 01:00:22,036
They lose reality.

689
01:00:22,119 --> 01:00:26,040
(STEVIE RAY VAUGHAN: <i>PRIDE AND JOY)</i>

690
01:00:27,374 --> 01:00:29,168
Joseph, what the hell
are you doing?

691
01:00:30,002 --> 01:00:32,129
Oh, my God.

692
01:00:32,213 --> 01:00:35,007
You don't believe.
I'll show you, you can't get hurt.

693
01:00:35,591 --> 01:00:39,136
That gun's not loaded. He doesn't know
where I keep the bullets.

694
01:00:39,220 --> 01:00:41,096
In your Rookie of the Year trophy.

695
01:00:42,139 --> 01:00:44,016
Joseph, did you load that gun?

696
01:00:45,017 --> 01:00:46,727
You won't get hurt.

697
01:00:46,811 --> 01:00:50,105
- Elijah was wrong.
- When did he meet Elijah?

698
01:00:50,189 --> 01:00:53,484
- He was with me when I met him.
- No one believes him.

699
01:00:53,567 --> 01:00:55,110
Joseph, listen to me.

700
01:00:56,529 --> 01:00:59,114
Sometimes when people
get sick or hurt

701
01:00:59,198 --> 01:01:03,077
for a long time, like Elijah,
their mind gets hurt too,

702
01:01:03,994 --> 01:01:05,996
and they start to think things
that aren't true.

703
01:01:06,080 --> 01:01:09,667
He told me what he thought
about your father. It isn't true.

704
01:01:09,750 --> 01:01:11,043
I'll show you.

705
01:01:14,129 --> 01:01:16,465
Do you remember the story
about the boy

706
01:01:16,549 --> 01:01:18,259
that almost drowned in the pool?

707
01:01:18,342 --> 01:01:21,011
That was me they were
talking about. I almost died.

708
01:01:21,095 --> 01:01:22,721
- You're lying.
- I'm not lying.

709
01:01:22,805 --> 01:01:24,056
I just didn't remember it.

710
01:01:24,139 --> 01:01:28,727
You know your father was injured in
college. You know. You know about that.

711
01:01:30,145 --> 01:01:31,188
Don't do it.

712
01:01:31,564 --> 01:01:34,149
- He'll die, Joseph.
- I'll just shoot him once.

713
01:01:34,233 --> 01:01:36,569
Joseph, listen to what your mot...

714
01:01:37,236 --> 01:01:39,196
Don't be scared.

715
01:01:40,698 --> 01:01:42,491
Joseph,

716
01:01:42,575 --> 01:01:45,035
if you pull that trigger,
I'm going to leave.

717
01:01:45,119 --> 01:01:48,455
Do you understand?
I'm going to go to New York.

718
01:01:48,539 --> 01:01:50,499
You're right.

719
01:01:50,583 --> 01:01:53,752
If you pull that trigger,
that bullet will bounce off me,

720
01:01:53,836 --> 01:01:57,006
and I'm not going to be hurt,
but then I'm going to go upstairs

721
01:01:57,089 --> 01:02:00,926
and I'm going to pack and
I'm going to leave for New York.

722
01:02:01,010 --> 01:02:02,136
Why?

723
01:02:02,219 --> 01:02:05,055
Because I thought we were just
starting to be friends for real,

724
01:02:06,140 --> 01:02:09,018
and friends will listen to each other.
They don't...

725
01:02:09,101 --> 01:02:12,021
And they don't shoot each other,
do they, Audrey?

726
01:02:12,104 --> 01:02:15,149
No shooting friends, Joseph.

727
01:02:15,482 --> 01:02:17,026
Joseph...

728
01:02:18,110 --> 01:02:20,821
...you are about to be
in big trouble!

729
01:02:22,323 --> 01:02:24,366
Now, I am your father,
and I am telling you

730
01:02:24,450 --> 01:02:27,620
to put that goddamn gun
down right now!

731
01:02:27,703 --> 01:02:29,622
One!

732
01:02:29,872 --> 01:02:31,707
- Two!
- (GASPS)

733
01:02:49,642 --> 01:02:51,727
<i>I followed the guy
in the camouflage jacket.</i>

734
01:02:53,896 --> 01:02:56,482
He had a silver gun
with a black grip

735
01:02:56,565 --> 01:02:58,984
tucked in the back of his pants.

736
01:03:00,861 --> 01:03:03,614
Were you really injured
in that car accident in college?

737
01:03:04,156 --> 01:03:05,991
Because I believe you faked it.

738
01:03:06,075 --> 01:03:09,286
I believe you took the opportunity
to end your football career,

739
01:03:09,370 --> 01:03:10,871
no questions asked.

740
01:03:10,955 --> 01:03:14,416
And I think you did it,
of all things, for a woman.

741
01:03:14,500 --> 01:03:16,085
I guess that makes sense.

742
01:03:16,669 --> 01:03:20,005
Football's, what, just ten years,

743
01:03:20,089 --> 01:03:21,674
but love...

744
01:03:21,757 --> 01:03:24,301
Hmm. Now, that's forever.

745
01:03:26,971 --> 01:03:30,140
And that little bit of sadness
in the mornings you spoke of,

746
01:03:30,224 --> 01:03:32,518
I think I know what that is.

747
01:03:32,601 --> 01:03:35,813
Perhaps you're not doing
what you're supposed to be doing.

748
01:03:35,896 --> 01:03:37,898
Most guns have
a black or silver handle.

749
01:03:37,982 --> 01:03:40,192
I had a 50-50 shot
of getting the color right.

750
01:03:40,275 --> 01:03:43,237
- That's not what I witnessed.
- Stop.

751
01:03:43,737 --> 01:03:46,657
Stop messing with my life, Elijah.

752
01:03:47,366 --> 01:03:49,034
My son almost shot me last night

753
01:03:49,118 --> 01:03:50,577
because he wanted to prove
you were right.

754
01:03:50,661 --> 01:03:52,454
I never said you couldn't be killed.

755
01:03:52,538 --> 01:03:54,456
- I never said that.
- My wife was right.

756
01:03:54,540 --> 01:03:57,251
There have been
three major disasters,

757
01:03:57,334 --> 01:03:59,420
and you were
the only one unharmed.

758
01:03:59,503 --> 01:04:01,422
I have been sick.

759
01:04:03,757 --> 01:04:06,176
When I was a kid,
I spent a week in the hospital

760
01:04:06,260 --> 01:04:09,888
getting better from pneumonia.
I almost drowned.

761
01:04:11,473 --> 01:04:14,435
Two skinny little kids were fooling
around in a pool dunking me,

762
01:04:14,518 --> 01:04:16,270
and I swallowed some water.

763
01:04:16,353 --> 01:04:18,355
They didn't know it,
and it almost killed me.

764
01:04:19,398 --> 01:04:21,734
Heroes don't get killed like that.

765
01:04:21,817 --> 01:04:23,736
Normal people do, right?

766
01:04:25,612 --> 01:04:28,198
I don't need to see you
anymore, OK?

767
01:04:30,284 --> 01:04:32,411
Please stay away from my family.

768
01:04:41,378 --> 01:04:44,965
(THE PIXIES: <i>I'VE BEEN TIRED)</i>

769
01:04:46,675 --> 01:04:49,219
Hey, man, it's 20 after.
It's time to choose.

770
01:04:49,303 --> 01:04:50,971
I gotta head.

771
01:04:55,434 --> 01:04:59,271
You better not be jacking off to
the Japanese comics, I swear to God.

772
01:05:12,701 --> 01:05:14,953
Look, man, I didn't know
you were in a...

773
01:05:17,372 --> 01:05:19,291
Just choose something, all right?

774
01:05:25,464 --> 01:05:27,382
The baby-sitter's here.

775
01:05:30,177 --> 01:05:33,263
- (WHISPERS) Should we cancel?
- I'm fine.

776
01:05:36,475 --> 01:05:39,103
We can do this another time.

777
01:05:39,186 --> 01:05:40,479
Don't lie.

778
01:05:43,398 --> 01:05:45,776
I'm fine. I just got mixed-up.

779
01:05:53,200 --> 01:05:55,869
How about we just go
for a couple drinks?

780
01:06:01,625 --> 01:06:04,169
OK, look, man,
I'm just gonna wheel you out.

781
01:06:04,253 --> 01:06:06,380
You can think about things
on the sidewalk.

782
01:06:06,463 --> 01:06:09,341
I got to get some chicken in me,
you know what I'm saying?

783
01:06:27,151 --> 01:06:29,236
Shit!

784
01:06:44,710 --> 01:06:46,003
Dude.

785
01:06:49,339 --> 01:06:53,343
I don't care if you are in a wheelchair.
If you do that again, I'm calling 5-0.

786
01:06:56,013 --> 01:06:57,264
Man.

787
01:07:08,358 --> 01:07:12,154
That's it, crackerjack. You're gonna
be sitting your ass in jail now.

788
01:07:15,908 --> 01:07:18,493
(DIALING PHONE)

789
01:07:34,426 --> 01:07:36,261
How much for this one?

790
01:07:39,223 --> 01:07:40,557
Rust?

791
01:07:40,641 --> 01:07:42,351
Yeah.

792
01:07:42,434 --> 01:07:44,978
As a color, not as rust.

793
01:07:45,062 --> 01:07:46,772
You know, like...

794
01:07:46,855 --> 01:07:49,399
...rust-colored paint or wood.

795
01:07:50,692 --> 01:07:52,611
- I didn't know that.
- Mm-hmm.

796
01:07:52,736 --> 01:07:54,196
Um...

797
01:07:54,279 --> 01:07:55,572
Mine's still brown.

798
01:07:56,907 --> 01:07:58,575
OK, my turn.

799
01:07:58,659 --> 01:08:00,202
Favorite song?

800
01:08:01,453 --> 01:08:05,415
<i>Soft and Wet</i> by the Artist
Formerly Known as Prince.

801
01:08:05,499 --> 01:08:06,583
What?

802
01:08:07,876 --> 01:08:09,586
We're supposed to be honest.

803
01:08:10,754 --> 01:08:13,423
<i>Soft and Wet.</i>

804
01:08:13,507 --> 01:08:17,094
- That's very interesting.
- (CHUCKLES) OK, my turn.

805
01:08:18,720 --> 01:08:21,890
- (CHUCKLES)
- When was the first time...

806
01:08:22,975 --> 01:08:25,602
...the thought popped into your head
we might not make it?

807
01:08:27,562 --> 01:08:29,439
That's not the game.

808
01:08:29,523 --> 01:08:32,150
It's the first date.
There aren't any rules.

809
01:08:33,402 --> 01:08:34,778
(EXHALES DEEPLY)

810
01:08:37,072 --> 01:08:38,615
Don't know for sure.

811
01:08:38,699 --> 01:08:39,992
Think carefully.

812
01:08:41,493 --> 01:08:43,495
What about the game?

813
01:08:43,578 --> 01:08:45,497
It's over. I won.

814
01:08:47,582 --> 01:08:49,835
Look, maybe it wasn't
a specific moment.

815
01:08:49,918 --> 01:08:51,336
Maybe it was a time...

816
01:08:51,420 --> 01:08:56,717
I had a nightmare one night, and...

817
01:08:56,800 --> 01:09:00,095
I didn't wake you up so you
could tell me that it was OK.

818
01:09:02,014 --> 01:09:04,016
I think that was the first time.

819
01:09:06,435 --> 01:09:07,769
Does that count?

820
01:09:10,022 --> 01:09:11,565
That counts.

821
01:09:15,610 --> 01:09:19,072
Do you knowingly keep me
and Joseph at a distance?

822
01:09:27,164 --> 01:09:28,623
Yes.

823
01:09:29,666 --> 01:09:31,543
Why?

824
01:09:33,962 --> 01:09:35,630
I don't know.

825
01:09:40,761 --> 01:09:43,347
I just don't feel right, Audrey.

826
01:09:44,431 --> 01:09:45,682
Something's...

827
01:09:46,808 --> 01:09:48,352
...just not right.

828
01:09:51,188 --> 01:09:52,814
Do you resent us, David?

829
01:09:54,566 --> 01:09:56,860
Resent the life you have?

830
01:09:58,737 --> 01:10:02,699
There were a lot of other things
you could've done after college.

831
01:10:02,783 --> 01:10:04,701
These were your choices.

832
01:10:09,498 --> 01:10:12,125
Even if it meant
we couldn't have been together,

833
01:10:12,209 --> 01:10:14,127
I never would've wished
that injury on you.

834
01:10:15,587 --> 01:10:19,549
What you could do physically
was a gift.

835
01:10:20,550 --> 01:10:23,595
I never would've wished it
to go away.

836
01:10:25,680 --> 01:10:27,599
You know that, right?

837
01:10:31,728 --> 01:10:33,897
You had two calls
after Joseph went to bed.

838
01:10:33,980 --> 01:10:36,733
One came through while I was on the
other line, but I wasn't talking long.

839
01:10:36,817 --> 01:10:40,737
There was an emergency with my sister.
She tried to do a perm, and she looks...

840
01:10:40,821 --> 01:10:42,948
Who called through?

841
01:10:43,031 --> 01:10:44,074
Um...

842
01:10:44,157 --> 01:10:47,619
Someone from New York,
about a security job.

843
01:10:47,702 --> 01:10:48,954
They want to hire you.

844
01:10:49,037 --> 01:10:51,331
I didn't know you guys
were moving to New York.

845
01:10:51,415 --> 01:10:52,874
Thanks for telling me.

846
01:10:53,333 --> 01:10:56,044
We weren't all gonna move.

847
01:10:57,254 --> 01:10:58,463
Oh.

848
01:11:01,341 --> 01:11:04,719
I let the answering machine
pick up the other call.

849
01:11:06,555 --> 01:11:08,557
(RAIN FALLING OUTSIDE)

850
01:11:08,640 --> 01:11:09,683
Great.

851
01:11:22,112 --> 01:11:26,116
Look, let's just be honest here.
We're just at the beginning,

852
01:11:26,199 --> 01:11:29,119
and I don't expect you or I to change

853
01:11:29,202 --> 01:11:31,663
the course of where our lives
were headed

854
01:11:31,746 --> 01:11:33,373
because of one date.

855
01:11:34,207 --> 01:11:36,126
And if you do go to New York,

856
01:11:36,209 --> 01:11:41,214
we can develop this.
We'll just be forced to take it slow.

857
01:11:41,715 --> 01:11:45,677
And in the end,
I think that's definitely better.

858
01:11:47,762 --> 01:11:49,681
This is our second time around, David.

859
01:11:49,764 --> 01:11:53,059
I didn't expect us to get
carried away, you know.

860
01:11:55,103 --> 01:11:58,565
Then I guess "congratulations"
is the right thing to say.

861
01:12:22,881 --> 01:12:26,176
- (BEEPS)
<i>- David, it's Elijah.</i>

862
01:12:26,259 --> 01:12:30,222
<i>It was so obvious. It was this one issue
that brought it back for me...</i>

863
01:12:30,305 --> 01:12:34,142
<i>Century Comics 117. That's where
this group, the Coalition of Evil,</i>

864
01:12:34,226 --> 01:12:37,479
<i>tried to ascertain the weakness
of every superhero,</i>

865
01:12:37,562 --> 01:12:40,815
<i>because they all have one,
just like you.</i>

866
01:12:40,899 --> 01:12:43,777
<i>Your bones don't break. Mine do.</i>

867
01:12:43,860 --> 01:12:44,986
<i>That's clear.</i>

868
01:12:45,070 --> 01:12:48,657
<i>Your cells react to bacteria
and viruses differently than mine.</i>

869
01:12:48,740 --> 01:12:50,867
<i>You don't get sick. I do.</i>

870
01:12:50,951 --> 01:12:52,786
<i>That's also clear.</i>

871
01:12:52,869 --> 01:12:54,204
<i>But for some reason,</i>

872
01:12:54,287 --> 01:12:57,958
<i>you and I react
the exact same way to water.</i>

873
01:12:58,041 --> 01:13:00,418
<i>We swallow it too fast, we choke.</i>

874
01:13:00,502 --> 01:13:02,754
<i>We get some in our lungs, we drown.</i>

875
01:13:02,837 --> 01:13:07,801
<i>However unreal it may seem,
we are connected, you and I.</i>

876
01:13:07,884 --> 01:13:10,053
<i>We're on the same curve,</i>

877
01:13:10,136 --> 01:13:12,681
<i>just on opposite ends.</i>

878
01:13:12,764 --> 01:13:16,893
<i>The point of all this is we now know
something we didn't.</i>

879
01:13:16,977 --> 01:13:19,062
<i>You have a weakness...</i>

880
01:13:19,145 --> 01:13:20,564
<i>...water.</i>

881
01:13:20,647 --> 01:13:22,774
<i>It's like your kryptonite.</i>

882
01:13:23,608 --> 01:13:25,527
<i>You hearing me, David?</i>

883
01:13:26,778 --> 01:13:27,946
(BEEPS)

884
01:15:16,137 --> 01:15:18,890
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

885
01:15:34,489 --> 01:15:35,657
(GRUNTS)

886
01:15:40,912 --> 01:15:42,205
Audrey?

887
01:16:07,272 --> 01:16:09,733
- (METAL CREAKING)
- (STRAINING)

888
01:16:16,364 --> 01:16:17,907
Gotcha.

889
01:16:17,991 --> 01:16:19,534
Come on.

890
01:16:36,134 --> 01:16:37,135
Audrey.

891
01:16:38,219 --> 01:16:39,220
Audrey.

892
01:16:45,018 --> 01:16:46,561
Hey!

893
01:16:50,982 --> 01:16:52,609
Audrey.

894
01:16:54,152 --> 01:16:55,403
What happened?

895
01:16:56,488 --> 01:16:58,072
What happened?

896
01:16:58,156 --> 01:16:59,365
I thought I was...

897
01:16:59,449 --> 01:17:00,992
I thought I was dead.

898
01:17:02,076 --> 01:17:03,369
Me too.

899
01:17:06,664 --> 01:17:08,541
(TRUCK DOOR SLAMS SHUT)

900
01:17:08,625 --> 01:17:10,418
Is she all right?

901
01:17:10,502 --> 01:17:12,212
I think her leg's broken.

902
01:17:13,046 --> 01:17:14,297
Are you hurt?

903
01:17:15,632 --> 01:17:17,133
Hey, man, are you hurt?

904
01:17:32,941 --> 01:17:35,151
(PHONE RINGING)

905
01:17:48,456 --> 01:17:50,083
Hello?

906
01:17:50,166 --> 01:17:53,002
DAVID: <i>I wasn't injured
in that car accident.</i>

907
01:17:53,086 --> 01:17:54,212
David.

908
01:17:55,839 --> 01:17:57,966
<i>I've never been injured, Elijah.</i>

909
01:18:00,844 --> 01:18:02,428
<i>What am I supposed to do?</i>

910
01:18:08,852 --> 01:18:11,145
Go to where people are.

911
01:18:11,896 --> 01:18:15,149
<i>You won't have to look very long.</i>

912
01:18:16,526 --> 01:18:19,612
<i>It's all right to be afraid, David,</i>

913
01:18:19,696 --> 01:18:22,156
<i>because this part
won't be like a comic book.</i>

914
01:18:23,700 --> 01:18:28,329
<i>Real life doesn't fit into little boxes
that were drawn for it.</i>

915
01:19:06,284 --> 01:19:08,453
There's one in the first row.

916
01:19:08,536 --> 01:19:11,122
It's... it's the second one.

917
01:19:11,205 --> 01:19:13,833
Yes. It's the second one over.

918
01:19:46,741 --> 01:19:49,577
- (SCREAMS)
- Go back to Africa!

919
01:19:49,661 --> 01:19:51,454
(TIRES SCREECH)

920
01:20:05,635 --> 01:20:06,970
Hey, what's your name?

921
01:20:13,393 --> 01:20:15,436
I think you drank too much.

922
01:21:01,315 --> 01:21:03,359
Can I come in?

923
01:21:03,443 --> 01:21:04,986
Who are you?

924
01:21:06,070 --> 01:21:07,905
I like your house.

925
01:21:08,948 --> 01:21:10,825
Can I come in?

926
01:21:10,908 --> 01:21:12,452
What is this?

927
01:21:14,912 --> 01:21:17,040
No. You can't come in.

928
01:21:18,249 --> 01:21:19,792
Are you sure?

929
01:21:20,877 --> 01:21:22,378
What... what are you...?

930
01:21:23,129 --> 01:21:25,089
(ECHOING SHRIEKS)

931
01:23:47,148 --> 01:23:50,902
(INDISTINCT CHATTER ON TELEVISION)

932
01:23:58,826 --> 01:24:00,494
(CLATTERING)

933
01:24:18,471 --> 01:24:19,931
(ECHOING SCREAMS)

934
01:24:51,462 --> 01:24:55,967
- (FOOTSTEPS)
- (CLATTERING)

935
01:25:26,998 --> 01:25:29,333
(SOBBING)

936
01:25:39,510 --> 01:25:41,512
(WHIMPERING SOFTLY)

937
01:25:44,265 --> 01:25:46,350
(SOBBING SOFTLY)

938
01:25:51,689 --> 01:25:54,692
- (PANTING)
- It's OK.

939
01:25:58,696 --> 01:26:00,865
(BOTH SOBBING SOFTLY)

940
01:26:05,536 --> 01:26:07,455
It's OK. It's OK.

941
01:26:19,550 --> 01:26:22,553
Shh, shh, shh...

942
01:26:29,727 --> 01:26:31,228
(DOOR OPENS)

943
01:26:32,646 --> 01:26:34,231
(THUNDER RUMBLING)

944
01:27:33,707 --> 01:27:35,793
(COUGHING, GASPING)

945
01:28:59,376 --> 01:29:01,128
(GASPS)

946
01:30:01,355 --> 01:30:02,773
(SHOUTING)

947
01:30:06,777 --> 01:30:08,445
(GRUNTING)

948
01:30:28,299 --> 01:30:29,800
(CHOKING)

949
01:30:43,814 --> 01:30:44,982
(GRUNTS)

950
01:30:47,318 --> 01:30:48,485
(GRUNTS)

951
01:32:51,275 --> 01:32:53,277
I had a bad dream.

952
01:32:59,908 --> 01:33:01,243
It's over now.

953
01:33:29,980 --> 01:33:31,523
DAVID: I don't know.

954
01:33:32,941 --> 01:33:34,943
I can't believe you'd think that.

955
01:33:35,027 --> 01:33:37,029
I'm just asking you.

956
01:33:40,240 --> 01:33:42,159
I'm making French toast.

957
01:33:58,091 --> 01:34:00,052
I've been thinking about Elijah Price.

958
01:34:01,804 --> 01:34:05,599
If he shows up again, I think
we should call the police, OK?

959
01:34:09,561 --> 01:34:10,687
OK?

960
01:34:14,107 --> 01:34:15,108
OK.

961
01:35:24,219 --> 01:35:26,138
(WHISPERS) You were right.

962
01:35:32,060 --> 01:35:33,228
(SNIFFLES)

963
01:35:50,120 --> 01:35:54,458
- (CLASSICAL MUSIC PLAYING)
- (CHATTERING)

964
01:36:15,938 --> 01:36:19,358
This is one of Johann Davis'
earliest drawings.

965
01:36:20,275 --> 01:36:22,486
See the villain's eyes?

966
01:36:22,569 --> 01:36:25,072
They're larger
than the other characters'.

967
01:36:25,155 --> 01:36:28,367
They insinuate a slightly
skewed perspective

968
01:36:28,492 --> 01:36:31,495
on how they see the world...

969
01:36:31,578 --> 01:36:33,872
...just off normal.

970
01:36:33,956 --> 01:36:35,582
He doesn't look scary.

971
01:36:36,208 --> 01:36:41,296
Mm-hmm. That's what I said to my son,
but he says there's always two kinds.

972
01:36:41,838 --> 01:36:43,674
There's the soldier villain

973
01:36:43,757 --> 01:36:47,719
who fights the hero with his hands,
and then there's the real threat,

974
01:36:47,803 --> 01:36:50,681
the brilliant and evil archenemy

975
01:36:50,764 --> 01:36:53,308
who fights the hero with his mind.

976
01:36:53,392 --> 01:36:56,770
- Are you Elijah's mother?
- I am.

977
01:36:56,853 --> 01:36:59,147
I'm helping him with the sale.

978
01:36:59,231 --> 01:37:01,984
Nice to meet you.
I'm David Dunn.

979
01:37:02,067 --> 01:37:04,027
He's spoken of you.

980
01:37:04,111 --> 01:37:07,531
- Says you're becoming friends.
- We are.

981
01:37:08,824 --> 01:37:10,742
Looks like he's doing good today.

982
01:37:12,119 --> 01:37:13,662
I'm very proud of him.

983
01:37:15,038 --> 01:37:17,124
He's come through a lot in his life,

984
01:37:18,333 --> 01:37:21,837
a couple of bad spills
I thought had broken him.

985
01:37:21,920 --> 01:37:23,213
Mm-hmm.

986
01:37:23,296 --> 01:37:24,589
They were bad...

987
01:37:26,174 --> 01:37:28,635
...but he made it.

988
01:37:28,719 --> 01:37:30,303
Yes, he did.

989
01:37:31,930 --> 01:37:34,224
He's kind of a miracle.

990
01:37:36,268 --> 01:37:37,811
Yes, he is.

991
01:37:41,648 --> 01:37:44,484
- I'll tell him you're here.
- Thank you.

992
01:38:14,431 --> 01:38:16,349
It has begun.

993
01:38:19,644 --> 01:38:21,563
Tell me something, David.

994
01:38:22,939 --> 01:38:25,233
When you woke up this morning,

995
01:38:25,317 --> 01:38:27,527
was it still there?

996
01:38:27,611 --> 01:38:29,154
The sadness?

997
01:38:30,322 --> 01:38:31,948
No.

998
01:38:33,200 --> 01:38:34,367
(EXHALES)

999
01:38:36,161 --> 01:38:38,789
I think this is
where we shake hands.

1000
01:38:54,554 --> 01:38:57,224
- (EXPLOSION)
- (GASPING)

1001
01:38:59,768 --> 01:39:01,436
Did you see it?

1002
01:39:04,481 --> 01:39:08,318
- (SIRENS WAILING)
- (ALARM BUZZING)

1003
01:39:10,403 --> 01:39:13,240
I worked in that building 25 years.

1004
01:39:13,323 --> 01:39:15,617
I know all its secrets.

1005
01:39:16,701 --> 01:39:18,120
Secrets?

1006
01:39:20,413 --> 01:39:25,085
Like if there ever was a fire
on floors one, two or three,

1007
01:39:25,168 --> 01:39:27,796
everyone in that hotel
would be burned alive.

1008
01:39:27,879 --> 01:39:29,714
- (SIRENS WAILING)
- (ALARM BUZZING)

1009
01:39:34,803 --> 01:39:37,347
Passengers aren't allowed in there.

1010
01:39:38,348 --> 01:39:40,517
(TRAIN RATTLING)

1011
01:39:44,354 --> 01:39:45,522
(GASPS)

1012
01:40:20,640 --> 01:40:22,976
ELIJAH: Do you know
what the scariest thing is?

1013
01:40:26,813 --> 01:40:29,024
To not know your place in this world,

1014
01:40:30,358 --> 01:40:35,155
to not know why you're here.
That's...

1015
01:40:38,116 --> 01:40:40,076
That's just an awful feeling.

1016
01:40:41,661 --> 01:40:43,705
What have you done?

1017
01:40:45,123 --> 01:40:47,167
I almost gave up hope.

1018
01:40:48,376 --> 01:40:51,588
There were so many times
I questioned myself.

1019
01:40:51,671 --> 01:40:54,174
You killed all those people.

1020
01:40:55,467 --> 01:40:57,344
But I found you.

1021
01:40:58,470 --> 01:41:01,556
So many sacrifices...

1022
01:41:02,933 --> 01:41:04,809
...just to find you.

1023
01:41:04,893 --> 01:41:06,645
Jesus Christ.

1024
01:41:16,738 --> 01:41:18,990
Now that we know who you are,

1025
01:41:20,533 --> 01:41:22,494
I know who I am.

1026
01:41:26,915 --> 01:41:29,167
I'm not a mistake.

1027
01:41:30,627 --> 01:41:32,796
It all makes sense.

1028
01:41:32,879 --> 01:41:36,549
In a comic, you know how you can tell
who the arch-villain's going to be?

1029
01:41:37,717 --> 01:41:41,179
He's the exact opposite of the hero,

1030
01:41:41,263 --> 01:41:44,766
and most times, they're friends,
like you and me.

1031
01:41:44,849 --> 01:41:47,978
I should've known way back when.
You know why, David?

1032
01:41:48,853 --> 01:41:50,772
Because of the kids.

1033
01:42:00,949 --> 01:42:03,660
They called me Mr. Glass.

